<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Peace of Thorn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1466">1466</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Toruń (1466) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirteen-years-war-1454-1466">Thirteen Years' War (1454–1466)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Peace of Thorn, signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> in October 1466, ended the Thirteen Years' War between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, with Danzig and the Order's former capital Marienburg, became Royal Prussia under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Polish crown</a>, together with the bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Culm</a>. The rest of the Order's state was held as a Polish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-peace-of-thorn.jpg" alt="Second Peace of Thorn" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marian Jaroczyński (from 1873) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2nd_peace_of_torun.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Thirteen Years' War, the longest of the Polish–Teutonic Wars, began in February 1454 when the Prussian Confederation, led by the cities of Danzig (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Gdańsk</a>), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a> (Elbląg), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Kulm</a> (Chełmno) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torun">Toruń</a> together with the Prussian gentry, rose against the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> in order to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a>. In the end both sides had exhausted their money and sought a negotiated peace. Talks opened at Nieszawa on 23 September 1466 and moved to Toruń for their last stages. The treaty was signed by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casimir-iv-jagiellon">Casimir IV Jagiellon</a> and the Order in the Artus Court on 19 October, followed by a mass in the Franciscan Church of the Assumption.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The Order renounced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Gdańsk Pomerania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno-land">Chełmno Land</a>, and recognised the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elblag">Elbing</a> and Marienburg (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a>) and the Bishopric of Warmia as Polish. The Diocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a> became a suffragan of the Archdiocese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gniezno">Gniezno</a>. Each Grand Master had to swear allegiance to the Polish king within six months of taking office and became a prince and counsellor of the king.</p><p>Any lands the Order gained, even outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, would belong to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> were to be admitted to the Order; the knights had to help Poland in war and could not fight Catholics without the king's consent. Prisoners were released. Both sides agreed to seek confirmation from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-paul-ii">Pope Paul II</a> and Emperor Frederick III, but the treaty ruled out any revision by a foreign authority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The lands held directly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> formed the province of Royal Prussia, made up of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a>, Pomeranian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malbork">Malbork</a> voivodeships and the Prince-Bishopric of Warmia. It kept its own coinage, Diet, military and use of German in administration, and a dispute over the choice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> in Warmia led to the War of the Priests (1467–1479). Royal Prussia kept distinctive features until the partitions of Poland. In 1525 Grand Master Albert Hohenzollern adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and became hereditary duke under Polish overlordship in the Prussian Homage, creating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/duchy-of-prussia">Duchy of Prussia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Peace of Thorn (1466)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The war that followed ended in October 1466 with the <b>Second Peace of Thorn</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Second Peace of Thorn</b> in 1466 handed Pomerelia, the Kulmerland, Marienburg, Elbing and Ermland to Poland as Royal Prussia, while the Order kept eastern Prussia as a fief, its Grand Master obliged to swear allegiance to the Polish king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Thirteen Years' War, the Prussian estates rebelled against the Order, and the <b>Second Peace of Thorn</b> in 1466 placed the Chełmno land under the Polish crown as part of Royal Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-culm">Bishopric of Culm</a></li><li><blockquote>Together, Poland and Lithuania crushed the Teutonic Knights at Grunwald in 1410, and the <b>Second Peace of Thorn</b> in 1466 brought Royal Prussia and the port of Gdansk under the Crown.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-poland-1385-1569">Kingdom of Poland (1385-1569)</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Second Peace of Thorn</b> in 1466 Warmia came under the protection of the Polish Crown, keeping wide self-government as part of Royal Prussia.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-warmia">Prince-Bishopric of Warmia</a></li></ul></section>
